{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"DOI:\"10.1073/pnas.2112672118\"","resultType":"core","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":25,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"34845026","source":"MED","pmid":"34845026","pmcid":"PMC8670470","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC8670470"]},"doi":"10.1073/pnas.2112672118","title":"Kinematic self-replication in reconfigurable organisms.","authorString":"Kriegman S, Blackiston D, Levin M, Bongard J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Kriegman S","lastName":"Kriegman","firstName":"Sam","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9207-1775"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Allen Discovery Center, Tufts University, Medford, MA 02155."},{"affiliation":"Wyss Institute for Biologically Inspired Engineering, Harvard University, Boston, MA 02115."}]}},{"fullName":"Blackiston D","lastName":"Blackiston","firstName":"Douglas","initials":"D","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7231-0196"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Allen Discovery Center, Tufts University, Medford, MA 02155."},{"affiliation":"Wyss Institute for Biologically Inspired Engineering, Harvard University, Boston, MA 02115."}]}},{"fullName":"Levin M","lastName":"Levin","firstName":"Michael","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7292-8084"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Allen Discovery Center, Tufts University, Medford, MA 02155."},{"affiliation":"Wyss Institute for Biologically Inspired Engineering, Harvard University, Boston, MA 02115."}]}},{"fullName":"Bongard J","lastName":"Bongard","firstName":"Josh","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Science, University of Vermont, Burlington, VT 05405 josh.bongard@uvm.edu."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7231-0196"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7292-8084"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9207-1775"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data","related_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"49","volume":"118","journalIssueId":3270119,"dateOfPublication":"2021 Dec","monthOfPublication":12,"yearOfPublication":2021,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A","title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","essn":"1091-6490","issn":"0027-8424","nlmid":"7505876","isoabbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A"},"printPublicationDate":"2021-12-01"},"pubYear":"2021","pageInfo":"e2112672118","abstractText":"All living systems perpetuate themselves via growth in or on the body, followed by splitting, budding, or birth. We find that synthetic multicellular assemblies can also replicate kinematically by moving and compressing dissociated cells in their environment into functional self-copies. This form of perpetuation, previously unseen in any organism, arises spontaneously over days rather than evolving over millennia. We also show how artificial intelligence methods can design assemblies that postpone loss of replicative ability and perform useful work as a side effect of replication. This suggests other unique and useful phenotypes can be rapidly reached from wild-type organisms without selection or genetic engineering, thereby broadening our understanding of the conditions under which replication arises, phenotypic plasticity, and how useful replicative machines may be realized.","affiliation":"Allen Discovery Center, Tufts University, Medford, MA 02155.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"DOD | Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency","grantId":"HR0011-180200022","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Paul G. Allen Frontiers Group","grantId":"12171","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshH